@page "/utility-scroll"

<CatHead Title="Scroll utilities">
    A region that is allowed to scroll on its own instead of growing the page. Both classes bring the
    library's scroll shadows with them, so the cut edge fades rather than ending abruptly.
</CatHead>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-stack-line"></i>
    <strong>The cascade layer is what makes these work.</strong> A utility is <code>(0,1,0)</code>
    and a component rule like <code>.table th, .table td</code> is <code>(0,1,1)</code>, so a utility
    loses on specificity alone. <code>sedna.utilities</code> sits after <code>sedna.paint</code>, so
    <code>.text-end</code> on a table cell wins regardless of it.
</div>

<div class="cat-note">
    <i class="ri-keyboard-line"></i>
    <strong>A scroll container needs to be reachable.</strong> Give it <code>tabindex="0"</code> and a
    name — <code>role="group"</code> with an <code>aria-label</code> — or a keyboard user
    cannot scroll it and a screen reader cannot say what it holds. Every demo below does.
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilityScroll.Vertical" Title="Vertical" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-scroll</code> caps a block's height and scrolls the overflow.
        <code>--sm</code>, <code>--md</code> and <code>--lg</code> are 12rem, 20rem and 32rem; for
        anything else set <code>--scroll-max</code> yourself, which is what the log block above does.
    </p>
    <p>
        The shadows read <code>--surface-here</code>, which every surface the library paints declares
        once, so a scroller inside a card, a modal, a drawer or a popover is already correct without
        being named.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilityScroll.Horizontal" Title="Horizontal" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-scroll-x</code> is the wrapper a wide table goes in, so the table scrolls sideways
        inside the page instead of the page scrolling sideways under the frame. Combine it with
        <code>.sedna-scroll</code> for both axes and the shadows on both edges.
    </p>
    <p>
        A table that is wide because it has too many columns is the other problem, and this does not
        solve it — see <a href="/table">Tables</a> for pinned columns and the stacked layout.
    </p>
</CatExample>
